  "raw": {
    "https://phx.us-west.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/9458F32E9190B17D00CF93BCC3CDE534/jobs/35457": {
      "title": "Instructor or Assistant Professor of Sport Management",
      "description": "Instructor or Assistant Professor of Sport Management You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Instructor or Assistant Professor of Sport Management Hot Job Mitchell College - New London, CT 06320 Apply Overview Salary Range $50,000.00 - $85,000.00 Salary/year Position Type Faculty Education Level Master's Degree Category Academic Year Full-Time Apply Description Mitchell College is building on its long history of making education more accessible by pioneering an approach which is flexible, strength-based, and interest-driven.  Our abilities-based approach to learning focuses on teaching adaptability through curriculum that provides multiple opportunities for students to apply learning in different classroom, social, and professional contexts.  To keep this model innovative, vibrant, and responsive to students’ needs and interests, we hire staff and faculty who are invested in neuro-inclusivity, collaboration, and community building.  Our approach to campus life ensures that students learn what they need to live life well, both professionally and personally. Mitchell College seeks to hire a student-centered early career professor to serve as Instructor or Assistant Professor of Sport Management.  The successful candidate will serve as curriculum subject matter expert for Sport Management in collaboration with faculty across the R. Carlson Division of Business. The Sport Management program is organized under the Business Division and Sport Management faculty report to the Division Chair. In addition to acting as curriculum subject matter expert, the successful candidate will lead the continued development of Sport Management as an applied, interdisciplinary program within the R. Carlson Division of Business. Working collaboratively with Faculty, Athletics, Enrollment Management, Career Services, and external partners, the new faculty member will help build a distinctive program that integrates business fundamentals, sport-industry knowledge, experiential learning, and career preparation. The position requires an educator who can deliver outstanding student experience in and out of the classroom, while driving the strategic growth, differentiation, and long-term sustainability of the program. Responsibilities: Teach 24 semester hours per academic year. As the division’s Sport Management subject matter expert, lead the review and redesign of Sport Management curriculum to ensure strong integration of business, management, marketing, finance, communication, data-informed decision-making, and emerging developments in the sport industry. Collaborate with Business and Hospitality faculty to develop shared courses, concentrations, certificates, and interdisciplinary pathways that reduce curricular duplication while expanding student choice. Assist the Division Chair in building the schedule of Sport Management classes each semester and in hiring and on-boarding part-time Sport Management faculty. Participate in student recruitment and retention activities as part of the Division of Business team. Build relationships with professional, collegiate, community, and youth sport organizations throughout southeastern Connecticut and the broader New England region. Work to achieve excellence in three criteria: Teaching & Mentoring, Professional Development Activities, College Involvement and Service to the Community. Fulfill the duties and responsibilities of a faculty member as defined in the Master Contract (American Federation of Teachers, Local #3249). Qualifications At the Rank of Assistant Professor: An earned doctorate in business, management, or a related field, from an accredited institution, along with a masters degree related to the business of sports. At the Rank of Instructor : A masters degree in sport management and current enrollment or future plans to enroll in an accredited doctoral program in business, management or a related field. Demonstrated successful teaching at the college level. Experience developing and revising curriculum. Experience assessing student learning outcomes. Work experience in a management or professional capacity, ideally related to the business of sports. Preference for candidates who have experience in and passion for working with diverse learners. The physical demands are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. A cover letter and CV are required. Application deadline is November 1, 2026. Projected start date is August 2027, with a potential early start date in January 2027 for the right candidate. Please note that this is a fully in-person position located in New London, Connecticut. Mitchell College is an Affirmative Action-Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate in employment on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, ancestry, political affiliation, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital status, disability and genetic information, age, membership in an employee organization or other non-merit factors. Share job details to About MITCHELL COLLEGE Hear from students, faculty, and staff how radical possibilities are realized at Mitchell College. Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://phx.us-west.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/9458F32E9190B17D00CF93BCC3CDE534/jobs/30073": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Nursing",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Nursing You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Assistant or Associate Professor of Nursing Hot Job Mitchell College - New London, CT 06320 Apply Overview Salary Range $70,000.00 - $95,000.00 Salary/year Position Type Faculty Education Level Master's Degree Category Academic Year Full-Time Apply Description Mitchell College invites applications for a full-time Assistant or Associate Professor of Nursing with expertise in adult health and medical-surgical nursing. This faculty member will join a small, collaborative team committed to strengthening and growing Mitchell’s prelicensure Bachelor of Science in Nursing program. The successful candidate will teach across didactic, laboratory, simulation, and clinical learning environments while contributing to curriculum development, student progression, program assessment, and continuous improvement. We seek an educator who combines current clinical knowledge with a highly relational approach to teaching and who is prepared to support students with varied educational experiences, learning profiles, and levels of academic preparation. This is an opportunity for a faculty member who wants to help shape a developing nursing program, work closely with students and colleagues, and contribute meaningfully to the program’s continued alignment with Connecticut Board of Examiners for Nursing requirements and Accreditation Commission for Education in Nursing standards. *Appointment at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank will be based on academic preparation, teaching experience, professional accomplishments, and Mitchell College’s criteria for faculty rank. Essential Responsibilities Teaching and student learning Teach undergraduate nursing courses in adult health and medical-surgical nursing across didactic, laboratory, simulation, and clinical settings. Use evidence-based and inclusive teaching practices that promote clinical judgment, safe practice, professional identity, and achievement of course and program outcomes. Integrate contemporary NCLEX-RN test-plan concepts, Next Generation NCLEX clinical-judgment approaches, and active-learning strategies throughout assigned courses. Evaluate student learning and clinical performance using clear, consistent, and appropriately rigorous assessment methods. Provide timely feedback, academic advising, clinical-skills support, and remediation to promote student progression and professional development. Curriculum and program assessment Participate in the development, implementation, and regular evaluation of the nursing curriculum. Contribute to curriculum mapping and the alignment of course outcomes, end-of-program student learning outcomes, professional nursing standards, and licensure expectations. Participate in the collection, analysis, and use of course and program data through the program’s systematic evaluation and continuous-improvement processes. Maintain accurate course, assessment, clinical, and accreditation documentation. Laboratory and simulation education Facilitate skills-laboratory and simulation experiences that reflect contemporary nursing practice and support the achievement of course outcomes. Develop or adapt simulation scenarios, learning activities, and evaluation methods appropriate to students’ level of preparation. Use evidence-based pre-briefing, facilitation, and debriefing practices. Collaborate with nursing colleagues to ensure the safe and effective use of simulation facilities, equipment, and technology. Program and College service Participate in nursing-program meetings, faculty governance, College committees, recruitment and orientation activities, and other institutional service. Contribute to ACEN accreditation activities and compliance with Connecticut nursing-education requirements. Collaborate with clinical partners, faculty colleagues, and College offices to support high-quality student learning experiences. Maintain currency in clinical practice, nursing education, assessment, and instructional technology through appropriate professional development. Engage in scholarly or professional activity appropriate to faculty rank and College expectations. Qualifications Required Qualifications Master of Science in Nursing from an appropriately accredited institution. Current, unencumbered RN license in Connecticut or eligibility to obtain Connecticut licensure before beginning employment. Professional nursing experience that supports teaching assignments in adult health or medical-surgical nursing. Experience teaching, precepting, mentoring, or evaluating nursing students or practicing nurses. Experience with laboratory, simulation, or clinical education. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively and work collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and clinical partners. Commitment to supporting students with varied backgrounds, educational experiences, and learning needs. Preferred Qualifications Earned doctorate in nursing, education, or a closely related field. College-level teaching experience in a prelicensure BSN program. Experience as a course coordinator or lead faculty member. Experience with curriculum development, program assessment, systematic evaluation, or nursing accreditation. Experience designing simulation scenarios and facilitating evidence-based debriefing. Familiarity with contemporary clinical-judgment frameworks and NCLEX-RN preparation. Certification as a nurse educator, simulation educator, or medical-surgical nurse. Share job details to About MITCHELL COLLEGE Hear from students, faculty, and staff how radical possibilities are realized at Mitchell College. Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://phx.us-west.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/9458F32E9190B17D00CF93BCC3CDE534/jobs/40191": {
      "title": "Administrative Coordinator",
      "description": "Administrative Coordinator You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Administrative Coordinator Mitchell College - New London, CT 06320 Apply Overview Salary Range $24.00 - $28.00 Hourly Position Type Staff Education Level High School/GED Category Regular Part-Time Apply Description Thames at Mitchell College seeks a highly organized, compassionate, and student-centered Administrative Assistant to provide administrative and operational support for a residential college-transition program serving neurodiverse students. The Administrative Assistant will help ensure that students, families, faculty, staff, and campus partners receive timely, accurate, and welcoming support. This role is ideal for someone who values inclusive education, understands neurodiversity as a natural part of human variation, and can balance attention to detail with a calm, respectful, relationship-based approach. The successful candidate will contribute to the daily functioning of Thames by coordinating communications, maintaining records and schedules, supporting program events and meetings, assisting with student-facing logistics, and helping the Thames team create an organized and affirming environment in which students can build confidence, independence, academic readiness, and community connection. Essential Responsibilities Administrative and Office Support Provide general administrative support to the Director of Thames and program staff. Manage routine correspondence, email communications, phone calls, mail, and inquiries from students, families, faculty, staff, and external partners. Maintain organized electronic and paper filing systems, including confidential student and program records. Assist with scheduling meetings, appointments, interviews, student check-ins, team meetings, and program events. Prepare meeting agendas, take notes or minutes as assigned, and distribute follow-up materials. Draft, format, proofread, and distribute letters, reports, newsletters, forms, presentations, handouts, and other program materials. Maintain calendars for program activities, events, deadlines, staff coverage, and important student-support milestones. Maintain office supplies, shared spaces, and administrative systems that contribute to an accessible and welcoming environment. Student, Family, and Program Support Serve as a warm, professional first point of contact for students, families, and visitors to Thames. Respond to routine questions and direct students and families to appropriate Thames staff, College offices, and campus resources. Assist with the logistical coordination of student orientation, family programs, workshops, advising activities, campus events, and community-building initiatives. Help prepare student-facing materials in clear, accessible, and supportive formats. Support communication reminders related to appointments, program expectations, events, deadlines, and campus resources. Assist with tracking student participation, attendance, appointments, and other program data, while maintaining appropriate confidentiality. Help create predictable, well-organized administrative processes that reduce unnecessary barriers for neurodiverse students. Support staff in preparing materials and logistics for student meetings, coaching sessions, workshops, and family engagement activities. Collaboration and Institutional Engagement Work collaboratively with Thames staff, Student Affairs, Academic Affairs, Residence Life, Accessibility Services, Counseling and Health Services, Enrollment, and other campus partners. Participate in relevant staff meetings, training programs, and professional-development opportunities. Support the preparation of materials for institutional reports, assessment efforts, recruitment activities, and program communications. Assist with data entry and administrative tasks connected to program evaluation, student success initiatives, retention efforts, and departmental planning. Uphold Mitchell College policies related to privacy, student records, professional conduct, equity, and inclusion. Commitment to Neurodiversity and Inclusion The Administrative Assistant should understand and support a strengths-based approach to neurodiversity. Thames serves students with a range of learning profiles, executive-functioning needs, communication styles, sensory preferences, and social-emotional experiences. The successful candidate will: Communicate with patience, clarity, and respect. Recognize that students may need information presented in multiple formats or repeated through supportive reminders. Help maintain predictable systems, routines, and expectations. Respect student dignity, autonomy, privacy, and individual differences. Contribute to a culture in which students are known as whole people rather than defined by diagnoses or challenges. Demonstrate cultural humility and a commitment to equity, accessibility, belonging, and student success. Qualifications Required Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent required; associate’s or bachelor’s degree preferred. At least two years of administrative, office-management, student-services, educational, or customer-service experience. Demonstrated experience working with neurodiverse individuals, students with learning differences, individuals with disabilities, or related communities. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Excellent organizational skills, attention to detail, and ability to manage multiple priorities. Proficiency with Microsoft Office or Google Workspace, including email, calendars, word processing, spreadsheets, and shared documents. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise sound judgment with sensitive student and family information. Ability to work independently while also contributing positively to a collaborative, team-based environment. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive practices and respectful engagement with people from varied backgrounds and identities. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, student affairs, disability services, college-transition programming, advising, residential life, or academic support. Familiarity with executive-functioning supports, including planning, organization, time management, task initiation, and follow-through. Experience supporting students and families during educational transitions. Familiarity with student-information systems, learning-management systems, CRM platforms, database entry, or event-management tools. Experience preparing accessible documents and communications. Training or coursework in special education, psychology, counseling, social work, higher education, disability studies, or a related field. Knowledge of FERPA and best practices for handling student records. Core Competencies Student-centered service and professional presence Organization, accuracy, and follow-through Clear and compassionate communication Confidentiality and sound judgment Flexibility and problem-solving Cultural humility and inclusive practice Collaborative teamwork Comfort with technology and administrative systems Appreciation for neurodiversity and strengths-based student support Work Environment and Physical Requirements This position is primarily campus-based and includes regular work in an office setting, with occasional participation in campus events, meetings, and student-program activities. The employee may be expected to use standard office equipment, move materials and supplies, and navigate campus spaces. Reasonable accommodations will be provided in accordance with applicable law and College policy. Equal Opportunity Statement Mitchell College is committed to fostering an inclusive community that values the dignity, experiences, identities, and contributions of all students, employees, and community members. The College encourages applications from individuals with diverse backgrounds and experiences, including individuals with disabilities and those with personal or professional experience supporting neurodiverse communities. Share job details to About MITCHELL COLLEGE Hear from students, faculty, and staff how radical possibilities are realized at Mitchell College. Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://phx.us-west.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/9458F32E9190B17D00CF93BCC3CDE534/jobs/37410": {
      "title": "Student Affairs Fellowship",
      "description": "Student Affairs Fellowship You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Student Affairs Fellowship Mitchell College - New London, CT 06320 Apply Overview Salary Range $20,000.00 - $20,000.00 Commission/year Position Type Fellowship Education Level Associate's Degree Category Seasonal/Temporary Apply Description The Mitchell College Fellowship Program is a developmental experience designed for emerging professionals interested in student affairs, higher education, and related fields. This program provides immersive, hands-on learning combined with mentorship, professional development, and intentional engagement across multiple functional areas of the college. Fellows gain broad exposure to campus operations and student success initiatives while developing skills in leadership, communication, student support, and program management. Rooted in Mitchell College’s commitment to building a Beloved Community, the Fellowship Program is designed to foster belonging, collaboration, and inclusive excellence while preparing Fellows for future careers in higher education. The experience also supports the Mitchell College Abilities framework by providing structured opportunities to develop professional competencies through applied practice, reflection, and assessment. This experience is intentionally designed to support professional exploration and career readiness in Student Affairs and related fields. Fellows are appointed for one-year terms, with the opportunity for re-appointment based on performance and organizational needs. Program Experience & Structure Fellows are placed within a primary department, which serves as their administrative base and supervision structure. Home departments may include: Residence Life Student Engagement Integrative Career Development Advancement and Alumni Affairs The Thames at Mitchell Program Enrollment Management Student Affairs In addition to their primary department, Fellows engage in cross-functional experiences across campus, providing meaningful exposure to multiple areas of higher education practice (secondary concentration/work groups). Assignments are designed to support professional growth, broaden institutional understanding, and strengthen transferable skills in student engagement, operations, and student success. Professional Development & Mentorship - Participate in a structured professional development curriculum designed to prepare emerging professionals for careers in higher education. - Engage in regular one-on-one mentorship with professional staff and supervisor. Student Engagement & Community Development - Support programs and initiatives that foster student engagement, belonging, and connection within Mitchell’s Beloved Community. - Participate in campus-wide signature events, traditions, and student success initiatives. - Contribute to efforts that enhance student retention, persistence, and overall student experience. Cross-Departmental Exposure - Gain experience across multiple functional areas within Student Affairs and institutional operations. - Collaborate with staff in various departments to support programming, services, and student support initiatives. - Develop a broad understanding of higher education operations and student success systems. Student Support & Responsibility - Provide support to students through engagement, outreach, and referral to appropriate resources. - Respond to student needs and assist in maintaining a safe, supportive, and inclusive campus environment. - Work with and support neurodivergent students, ensuring inclusive, accessible, and affirming engagement practices. Operations & Administrative Experience - Assist with program coordination, event logistics, documentation, and administrative support. - Contribute to assessment, reporting, and data-informed decision-making processes. - Support institutional initiatives that advance student success and engagement. Compensation & Benefits - Annual stipend: $20,000 - On-campus housing available (required for student life positions). - All live-on positions within student success participate in the on-call duty rotation to support campus safety and student well-being. - Meal plan provided during academic terms for student success live-on positions. - Eligibility for institutional tuition exchange programs Qualifications Associate’s degree or equivalent academic credit required 1–2 years of experience preferred, or demonstrated commitment to and engagement with work related to the primary department’s areas of responsibility. Strong communication, interpersonal, and organizational skills Commitment to inclusive excellence and contributing to Mitchell’s Beloved Community Ability to work a flexible job schedule including evenings, weekends to meet the needs of students. Willingness to learn new systems and engage in professional development Valid driver’s license required for roles involving driving campus vehicles. Physical Requirements This role may require sitting, standing, walking, and use of hands for computer-based tasks. Occasional lifting up to 50 pounds may be required. Reasonable accommodations will be provided as needed. Mitchell College is an Affirmative Action-Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate in employment on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, ancestry, political affiliation, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital status, disability and genetic information, age, membership in an employee organization or other non-merit factors. Share job details to About MITCHELL COLLEGE Hear from students, faculty, and staff how radical possibilities are realized at Mitchell College. Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://phx.us-west.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/9458F32E9190B17D00CF93BCC3CDE534/jobs/36223": {
      "title": "Substitute Teacher",
      "description": "Substitute Teacher You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Substitute Teacher Mitchell College - New London, CT 06320 Apply Overview Salary Range $20.00 - $22.00 Hourly Position Type Staff Education Level Bachelor's Degree Category Casual/Per-Diem Apply Description The Substitute Teacher is essential in ensuring that students continue to learn and grow every day by helping to create a caring and safe environment. This position assumes responsibilities and duties for the classroom and is responsible for all daily classroom operations and execution of learning plan.  The Substitute Teacher, along with other teaching staff, assist each other and Director in operating the center in accordance with Mitchell College policies, procedures and state licensing standards to provide an educational environment for children, staff and families that is nurturing and safe. In the absence of the Head Teacher, the Assistant Preschool Teacher. Responsibilities and Duties: Assists in the implementation of developmentally appropriate inquiry-based curriculum; knowledge of a Reggio Emilia Approach to learning is preferable. Infuses creativity into all aspects of the children’s educational day. Improves the physical environment in the classroom to create opportunities to meet the changing needs of the developing child. Provides responsive care to all children by adapting daily care giving routines and plans to the interests and needs of the individual child and the group. Demonstrates cultural competency and respect for the child's background by incorporating the cultural, linguistic and familial values and beliefs into the childcare program and lesson plans. Provides information and support to families whose children are in care including referrals to support agencies, parenting/family counseling resources and other counseling resources. May be asked to conducts tours and provide interested families with information about our program Understands and assists student workers and teacher’s assistants with developmentally appropriate practice in Early Childhood Education Complies with, and may implement OEC regulations regarding the care of children Informs the Director of any necessary information regarding the care and safety of children Assists and accepts temporary responsibility for other daily center duties in the event other personnel are not available Attends and participates in staff meetings, center events, and parent meetings as required Responds immediately to unusual or crisis situations Functions effectively in an inclusive work environment Qualifications Required Education and Experience: Bachelor’s Degree in Early Childhood Education, Child Studies, or Human Development Family Studies or related field preferred.  Transcripts are required prior to offer. One to two years of professional teaching experience Child CPR and First Aid certified preferred. Knowledge of CT-ELDS and a Reggio Emilia inspired approach to curriculum One year of general experience working in a group program with children and/or youth. Proof of a physical examination within the last six months, which includes a tuberculosis test, and comply with other background checks as required by the OEC/DPH licensing; Excellent communication and interpersonal skills and the ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with students, faculty, and parents. Ability to maintain confidential information and work independently with a high level of discretion Valid driver’s license Key Skills / Knowledge: Standard computer skills (Word, Excel, etc) Strong organizational skills Strong oral and written communication skills Excellent customer service skills Effective interpersonal skills Share job details to About MITCHELL COLLEGE Hear from students, faculty, and staff how radical possibilities are realized at Mitchell College. Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://phx.us-west.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/9458F32E9190B17D00CF93BCC3CDE534/jobs/5213": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Adjunct Faculty Mitchell College - New London, CT 06320 Quick Apply Overview Position Type Faculty Category Seasonal/Temporary Quick Apply Description Mitchell College in New London, CT regularly accepts PT adjunct faculty applications in all of the fields represented in the curriculum. You will be contacted when your application meets our upcoming needs. Mitchell College is an independent co-educational baccalaureate institution offering both associate and bachelor’s degrees in over 20 programs of study. The College is dedicated to providing a challenging education in a caring and cooperative student-centered environment. Qualifications A master’s degree is required; a terminal degree in the field of expertise is preferred Teaching experience in higher education essential Skill with instructional technologies and experience with on-line and blended learning instructional delivery is a plus and experience with Ability Based Education or competency-based education is helpful. Transcripts are required to be submitted as part of the application process. Mitchell College, founded in 1938, is a private, higher education institution that offers associate and bachelor degrees and is home to Thames at Mitchell, our holistic, innovative college onboarding program located on the campus of Mitchell College. Mitchell College does not discriminate in employment on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, ancestry, political affiliation, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital status, disability and genetic information, age, membership in an employee organization or other non-merit factors. Share job details to About MITCHELL COLLEGE Hear from students, faculty, and staff how radical possibilities are realized at Mitchell College. Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
